    "created_at": "2026-05-19T21:11:10Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-05-22T10:49:43Z",
    "body": "> It could probably be made to work, but it would require special care and it's not entirely clear if it's worth the effort. Consider if you had `opaque:path` and then you'd do `.pathname = \"//newpath\"`. The no-ops are usually because existing implementations already no-opped or did something bad.\n\nTheoretically you could just change leading / to %2F. There's already the thing where an opaque path's trailing space is only encoded if it's just before the query/fragment, even though always encoding it gives equivalent results with less complexity and further unblocks setting opaque paths.",
